---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { site } from "../../data/site";

// Stand-alone privacy policy for the mobile apps we publish on Google Play.
// This is the URL entered in Play Console's store listing, so it must be a
// complete policy on its own. The website has its own policy at /privacy
// (it runs analytics; this doesn't). Describes Delve, Die, Repeat 2.3: our
// own leaderboard on Google Firebase through the in-game Compete setting,
// shown under your Google Play Games name (Compete signs in with Google Play
// Games), ERASE MY RUNS, and in-app bug reports (☰ REPORT A BUG), sent only
// when the player taps Send, with a per-player cooldown and daily cap. The Play Console Data safety record lives in the game repo at
// store-listing/LISTING.md (mazeworld); the "Data safety summary" section
// below must match it.
const effective = "October 1, 2026";
// The address Google Play lists as the developer contact. Change here only.
const contact = "sheibeck@gmail.com";
---
<Layout
  title="Privacy Policy — Google Play apps"
  description="Privacy policy for Darktier Studios apps on Google Play, including Delve, Die, Repeat. Saves stay on your device; one small database on Google Firebase holds the public leaderboard and bug reports; the leaderboard shows your Google Play Games name; Compete can be turned off at any time; a bug report is sent only when you tap Send."
>
  <div class="container">
    <section style="padding:84px 0 28px">
      <p class="kicker">Darktier Studios · legal · Google Play apps</p>
      <h1 style="font-size:clamp(40px,6vw,72px);line-height:1.04;letter-spacing:-0.02em;margin:0 0 0 -0.06em">Privacy policy for our apps</h1>
      <p class="privacy-meta">Effective {effective} · {site.legalName}, {site.location}</p>
    </section>

    <article class="privacy">
      <p class="privacy-lede">
        <strong>We keep one small database on Google Firebase for the public leaderboard and bug reports, nothing else.</strong> Your saves, your run history, and your settings live on your device. The
        leaderboard runs through the in-game <strong>Compete</strong> setting, which is on by default and
        which you can turn off at any time — with Compete off, nothing is sent and you are not signed in to
        Google Play Games. A <strong>bug report</strong>
        leaves your phone only if you write one and tap <strong>Send</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Who this covers</h2>
      <p>
        This policy applies to the mobile apps published by {site.legalName} on Google Play — currently
        <strong>Delve, Die, Repeat</strong> (package <code>com.darktierstudios.delvedierepeat</code>).
        Our website and browser-based tools are covered separately by the
        <a href="/privacy">website privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Data we collect</h2>
      <p>
        {site.legalName} runs the one small database described above, and nothing else. The only personal
        information involved is the <strong>Google Play Games name</strong> you chose in Google Play Games,
        which appears on the leaderboard beside your runs (next section). Beyond that, our apps do not
        collect, transmit, sell, or share:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your real name, email address, phone number, or address (we never ask Google for them)</li>
        <li>Location data, precise or approximate</li>
        <li>Financial or payment information</li>
        <li>Contacts, calendar, photos, videos, audio, or files</li>
        <li>Gameplay analytics, or automatic crash and diagnostic reports from our own code</li>
        <li>Device identifiers or the Android Advertising ID</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The game sends data in only two cases: the leaderboard data described in the next section, only
        while Compete is on; and a bug report you write yourself, only when you tap Send, described under
        <a href="#bug-reports">Bug reports</a>. Google's own Play Games software, which runs while Compete
        is on, collects a little data of its own, described under
        <a href="#play-games">Google Play Games</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Our leaderboard (Compete)</h2>
      <p>
        Delve, Die, Repeat posts your finished runs to a public leaderboard — deepest descent, longest-lived,
        most kills, and fattest purse — so you can see where you placed against everyone playing, under your
        <strong>Google Play Games name</strong>. This runs through the in-game <strong>Compete</strong>
        setting, which is on by default, needs you to be signed in to Google Play Games, and which you can
        turn off at any time.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your Google Play Games name:</strong> the gamer name, a nickname, that you chose in Google
          Play Games. It appears publicly beside each of your runs on the leaderboard. You change it in
          Google Play Games, not in our game, and the board shows the new one after you sign in again. If a
          name contains a word from our safety list, the board shows a neutral placeholder instead, and we
          can hide a run. We never ask for the real name or email of your Google account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your game ID:</strong> a random Firebase account ID the game creates for you, which
          Firebase Authentication links to your Google Play Games player ID when you sign in with Compete on.
          The player ID is never shown to anyone else. The ID carries no email, phone number, device
          identifier, or advertising ID. It's created when Compete is on and you send your first run, or
          anonymously, with no link, when you send a bug report.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How the name gets onto the board:</strong> when you sign in, a Google Cloud Function in
          our one small Firebase project asks Google's sign-in service to confirm your Google Play Games
          name, and records it next to your game ID, so the board's names stay current and cannot be faked
          from a phone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The run details we send:</strong> for each run that ends while Compete is on, we send
          your game ID, your Google Play Games name, and the run's own details:
          <ul>
            <li>the season</li>
            <li>your adventurer's name (rolled by the game, not yours), race, class, and sub-class</li>
            <li>level, the floor you reached, the days you survived, the squares you walked, your kills, the
              wilmst you carried, and your experience</li>
            <li>your cause of death, what killed you, and your epitaph</li>
            <li>the time of death, the app version, the dungeon's random seed, and the turn count</li>
            <li>a fingerprint of the run, and the ranking numbers worked out from the fields above — not
              new information</li>
          </ul>
          Our database also records when it received the run.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>When it's sent:</strong> as each run ends, while Compete is on — queued on your phone and
          sent the next time you're online, and held until you are signed in to Google Play Games. Once
          only, the first time Delve, Die, Repeat 2.3 starts with Compete on, the game also re-sends runs
          from your own list of runs sent with Compete on that never reached the board, and your earlier
          2.2.0 runs move under your Google Play Games name.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Who sees it:</strong> the leaderboard is public. Any other player can see your Google
          Play Games name and your runs, including the epitaph and the cause of death.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Where it goes:</strong> our one small database on Google Firebase, which stores it for us
          as our service provider. We never sell it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>With Compete off:</strong> nothing is sent. No Google Play Games sign-in is asked for and
          the Play Games software is not started, no game ID is created, and any runs still waiting to be
          sent are thrown away. Runs you finish while Compete is off are never uploaded later, even if you
          turn Compete back on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Erasing your runs:</strong> <strong>ERASE MY RUNS</strong>, in the account block, deletes
          every run of yours on the board, in every season, deletes the board name we recorded for you, and
          deletes your game account together with its Google Play Games link. Your local run history stays
          on your phone. Signing in again and sending a run later starts a new game account under your
          Google Play Games name. To cut Google's side too, disconnect the game in Google Play Games
          settings.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Forged runs:</strong> we remove runs that look forged or tampered with, and we can stop a
          game ID from posting again.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Retention:</strong> your runs stay on the board until you erase them or we remove them.
          Runs from past seasons stay in our database after a season ends, unless you erase them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Finding the account block:</strong> in the dungeon, open the ☰ menu; on the title screen,
          tap the corner chip. From there: <strong>COMPETE</strong>, <strong>SIGN IN</strong> (when you are
          not signed in to Google Play Games), and <strong>ERASE MY RUNS</strong>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Versions before 2.2 posted scores to Google's own game leaderboards, which are being retired and
        deleted. Version 2.2.0 showed a rolled @name instead of a Google Play Games name; those runs stay
        on the board under that name until their owner updates and signs in, and then they move.
      </p>

      <h2 id="play-games">Google Play Games</h2>
      <p>
        With Compete on, the game signs you in with Google Play Games. We use it for sign-in only: no
        Google leaderboards, achievements, or saved games. Google's own Play Games software runs inside the
        app while Compete is on and collects data automatically: your gamer identity (gamertag and avatar,
        which our game never reads or shows), plus analytics and diagnostics data that Google uses to keep
        its software stable. That data goes to Google, not to us, under Google's own terms and privacy
        policy; Google describes it at
        <a href="https://developer.android.com/games/pgs/data-collection" target="_blank" rel="noopener">developer.android.com/games/pgs/data-collection</a>.
        With Compete off, the Play Games software is not started, so it collects nothing. You can delete
        your Play Games account and its data in your Play Games profile or Google MyAccount.
      </p>

      <h2 id="bug-reports">Bug reports</h2>
      <p>
        Delve, Die, Repeat has a <strong>REPORT A BUG</strong> option in its ☰ menu. Nothing is sent unless
        you type a report and tap <strong>Send</strong>. A report contains what you typed, the game's
        in-run log (the Oracle, which may include your adventurer's randomly generated name), the app
        version and build, your device model and Android version, and a summary of the current run (floor,
        class, race, level and turn count). It carries no account, email address, or other contact detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        Sending a report uses your anonymous game ID only as the key of a small limit record — the time of
        your last report, the day, and a count — that enforces a short cooldown and a daily cap on reports;
        that record is deleted automatically about 2 days after your last report. The report itself does
        not carry the game ID.
      </p>
      <p>
        Reports are stored in the game's Google Firebase project and posted publicly as issues on the
        game's GitHub repository
        (<a href="https://github.com/sheibeck/ddr" target="_blank" rel="noopener">github.com/sheibeck/ddr</a>),
        where anyone can read them, so please leave out anything private. A report is deleted from our
        database once it's filed (30 days at most either way), and the public GitHub issue is deleted on
        request — follow the steps on our <a href="/privacy/delete-data#bug-reports">delete your data</a>
        page or <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>contact us</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Data stored on your device</h2>
      <p>
        The game keeps a small amount of data on your phone so it works: your current run, your local run
        history (YOUR DEAD), your personal best records, your settings (including Compete), your game ID
        and Google Play Games name, and, while you are offline, the short list of runs waiting to be sent to the
        leaderboard. None of it is sent to us except as described above, and it is removed when you
        uninstall the app — except runs already on the leaderboard, which stay there until you erase them
        (see <a href="/privacy/delete-data">delete your data</a>).
      </p>

      <h2>One small database, no ads, no analytics</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps require no account of our own and play fully offline. They contain no advertising,
        analytics, or crash-reporting SDKs of our own. The only network calls our code makes reach Google
        Firebase over plain HTTPS — with no Firebase SDK bundled in the app — for the leaderboard, your
        board name, and bug reports, all described above; Google's Play Games software makes its own, as
        described above, only while Compete is on. Google Play handles your purchase and installation under
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Permissions</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps do not request access to your location, contacts, camera, microphone, storage, or any
        other permission-gated data. The one permission is internet access, used only for the leaderboard
        while Compete is on (sign-in and the board), and for sending a bug report when you tap Send.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        Our apps are suitable for general audiences. We collect no email addresses or other contact
        details from anyone, including children under 13. The only name the board shows is the gamer name
        a player chose in Google Play Games, which Google governs, including its supervised accounts, whose
        names Google generates; there is no chat or messaging of any kind. The game needs no sign-in to be
        played, and Compete, which is the only thing that uses Google Play Games, can be turned off at any
        time.
      </p>

      <h2>Your rights</h2>
      <p>
        We hold the runs you sent to the leaderboard while Compete was on, and any bug report you chose to
        send. <strong>ERASE MY RUNS</strong>, in the game, removes your leaderboard runs, your board name,
        and your game account yourself. Or email us with your Google Play Games name and we will delete them
        and confirm. Uninstalling the app deletes everything
        stored on your device. If a future app ever changes any of this, this policy will change first.
      </p>

      <h2>Data safety summary</h2>
      <p>
        For the Google Play Data safety section, Delve, Die, Repeat declares: <strong>Name</strong> (your
        Google Play Games name, shown publicly beside your runs) — collected, not shared, optional (turn
        Compete off), used for <strong>App functionality</strong>, encrypted in transit, and deletable with
        ERASE MY RUNS. <strong>User IDs</strong> (the game's Firebase account ID, linked to your Google Play
        Games player ID when you sign in with Compete on; the player ID is never shown) — collected, not
        shared, optional (created only when Compete is on and a run is sent, or when you send a bug
        report), App functionality, encrypted in transit, and deletable with ERASE MY RUNS.
        <strong>App activity</strong> (your Google Play Games name and the run details listed above, sent
        for each run while Compete is on) — collected, not shared, optional (turn Compete off), App
        functionality, encrypted in transit, deletable, and public on the leaderboard.
        <strong>Diagnostics</strong> and <strong>Photos</strong> collected by Google's own Play Games
        software while Compete is on (the data Google's page lists: analytics, diagnostics, and the avatar
        half of your gamer identity; our game never reads the avatar) — optional, because with Compete off
        the software never starts. <strong>Other user-generated content</strong> (the bug report you type, sent only when
        you tap Send) — collected, not shared, posted publicly on GitHub, deleted from our database once
        filed. <strong>Diagnostics</strong> (the Oracle log, app version and build, device model and
        Android version, and a summary of the run, attached to a bug report only) — collected, not shared,
        deleted on request. None of it is sold.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
      <p>
        If we update this policy, the new version will be posted at this address with a new effective date.
        Material changes to what an app collects will also be reflected in that app's Google Play listing.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about this policy: <a href={`mailto:${contact}`}>{contact}</a><br />
        {site.legalName} · {site.location}
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
